Chapter: TruckDrawSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter TruckDraw
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 TruckDraw: the box the whole truck is measured from, the axles hung off it, and
 the face sort that decides what paints over what.

 WHAT THIS GRADES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Every vertex of the truck goes through
 `at` and then drops by the ground curve, so a projected coordinate is Camera's
 answer and Render's frame, and TruckCheck already grades those against the game.
 What is TruckDraw's OWN is the arithmetic above the projection -- where the
 faces are in the body frame -- and the ordering below it. Both are reachable
 with no segment, no pose and no camera, which is why they are here rather than
 in a check that needs all three.

 THE SORT IS THE PART WORTH A TEST. It is a stable merge far-to-near over a
 comparison that DECLINES to order two depths the game cannot tell apart, and
 those are two separate properties that a rewrite can break one at a time. A
 plain `>` still passes a test that only asks for descending order; it fails the
 tie cases below. And an epsilon grown "to be safe" still passes the tie cases;
 it fails the 0.01 case beside them. Both directions are graded.

 WHERE THE EXPECTED VALUES COME FROM: the chapter's own arithmetic, worked
 through by hand. `truck-box` is four sums off a centre and two off a half-width,
 `truck-axles` is three abscissas and a pair gap, and the sort's answers are the
 build order the merge is defined to preserve. Nothing here was read off a run.

 THE CENTRE IS 0.0 AND 40.0 AND NEITHER IS ARBITRARY. Every value both produce
 needs at most five significant digits, so every literal below is one both arms
 convert to the same double. A centre of 100.0 puts `truck-axles` at
 97.99999999999999 and 102.00000000000001 -- seventeen digits, which is where
 Codex's decimal conversion stops being correctly rounded (upstream issue 125),
 and this spec would then fail on the zig arm for a reason that has nothing to do
 with the truck. Grading at 0.0 tolerance means choosing inputs whose answers are
 short, and that is a constraint on the test rather than on the chapter.

 We say:

Section: The Box

 `truck-box` places the trailer rear, the trailer front, the cab nose and the
 roof break along the road, and the two sides across it. The truck is 8.4 long
 and 2.4 wide, so the rear sits 4.2 behind the centre and each side 1.2 off the
 half-width; the cab adds 3.5 past the trailer front and its roof breaks three
 tenths of the way along that.

  box-at : Real, Real -> List Real
  box-at (c) (hw) =
    let b = truck-box c hw
    in [b.a0, b.a1, b.a2, b.a-roof, b.xl, b.xr]

  box-got : List Real
  box-got = box-at 0.0 0.0 & box-at 40.0 2.0

  box-want : List Real
  box-want =
    [ -4.2, 4.2, 7.7, 5.25, -1.2, 1.2,
      35.8, 44.2, 47.7, 45.25, 0.8, 3.2 ]

 The two heights the box does not carry, because nothing varies them: a tire is
 half a metre, the trailer floor clears a tire and a bit, and the cab floor sits
 on the axle line.

  bottom-got : List Real
  bottom-got = [tire-radius, trailer-bottom, cab-bottom, truck-height, truck-width, truck-length]

  bottom-want : List Real
  bottom-want = [0.5, 1.12, 0.5, 3.6, 2.4, 8.4]

Section: The Axles

 Five a side: a tandem pair inset 1.6 from each trailer end and straddling a 1.2
 gap, then one under the cab at 0.55 of the cab's length. The order is the order
 the tires are built in, and the tires join the face sort, so it is part of the
 answer rather than a convenience.

  axles-got : List Real
  axles-got = truck-axles (truck-box 0.0 0.0) & truck-axles (truck-box 40.0 2.0)

  axles-want : List Real
  axles-want = [-3.2, -2.0, 2.0, 3.2, 6.125, 36.8, 38.0, 42.0, 43.2, 46.125]

  axle-count-got : List Integer
  axle-count-got = [list-length (truck-axles (truck-box 0.0 0.0))]

  axle-count-want : List Integer
  axle-count-want = [5]

Section: A Face

 A face carries its colour, its polygon, and the MEAN depth it sorts by --
 computed once here where the zig recomputes it inside its comparison. The mean
 is over the vertices, so a three-vertex face and a four-vertex face are both
 divided by their own count.

  v3 : Real -> Vec3
  v3 (f) = Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = f, height = 0.0 }

  quad-face : TruckFace
  quad-face = truck-face 1846886 [v3 10.0, v3 20.0, v3 30.0, v3 40.0]

  tri-face : TruckFace
  tri-face = truck-face 1381658 [v3 1.0, v3 2.0, v3 6.0]

  face-got : List Real
  face-got = [quad-face.fwd, tri-face.fwd]

  face-want : List Real
  face-want = [25.0, 3.0]

 The colour and the polygon are carried through untouched.

  face-carry-got : List Integer
  face-carry-got = [quad-face.color, list-length quad-face.v, tri-face.color, list-length tri-face.v]

  face-carry-want : List Integer
  face-carry-want = [1846886, 4, 1381658, 3]

Section: The Sort

 Far to near, and STABLE: two faces the comparison cannot separate stay in the
 order they were built. Colours stand in for identity here so the order can be
 read off directly.

  mark : Integer, Real -> TruckFace
  mark (c) (f) = TruckFace { color = c, fwd = f, v = [] }

  colours-of : List TruckFace, Integer -> List Integer
  colours-of (fs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length fs then []
    else [(list-at fs i).color] & colours-of fs (i + 1)

 Three faces at exactly the same depth, interleaved with two others. The 30s come
 out first and in build order; nothing about their depths distinguishes them.

  tied : List TruckFace
  tied = [mark 1 30.0, mark 2 10.0, mark 3 30.0, mark 4 20.0, mark 5 30.0]

  tied-got : List Integer
  tied-got = colours-of (sort-faces tied) 0

  tied-want : List Integer
  tied-want = [1, 3, 5, 4, 2]

 THE SLACK, FROM BOTH SIDES. At a depth of 20 the comparison's threshold is 1.2
 microns of depth -- half an f32 ulp there -- so face 2 is a millionth deeper
 than face 1 and must NOT be ordered ahead of it, while face 3 is a hundredth
 deeper and must be. A plain strict `>` swaps 1 and 2; an epsilon large enough to
 be comfortable swallows 3.

  slack : List TruckFace
  slack = [mark 1 20.0, mark 2 20.000001, mark 3 20.01, mark 4 19.0]

  slack-got : List Integer
  slack-got = colours-of (sort-faces slack) 0

  slack-want : List Integer
  slack-want = [3, 1, 2, 4]

 A sort of nothing and a sort of one are the merge's base case.

  small-got : List Integer
  small-got =
    [ list-length (sort-faces []),
      list-length (sort-faces [mark 7 1.0]),
      (list-at (sort-faces [mark 7 1.0]) 0).color ]

  small-want : List Integer
  small-want = [0, 1, 7]

Section: The Halo

 Screen-space arithmetic, with no projection in it: the panel's centre is the
 mean of its four corners, the radius is the FARTHEST of them, and the gradient
 reaches 3.2 times that so the fill covers every vertex.

  sp : Real, Real -> ScreenPt
  sp (x) (y) = ScreenPt { x = x, y = y }

  panel : List ScreenPt
  panel = [sp 3.0 4.0, sp 6.0 8.0, sp 0.0 0.0, sp 7.0 4.0]

  halo-got : List Real
  halo-got =
    let cx = sum-x panel 0 / 4.0
    in let cy = sum-y panel 0 / 4.0
    in [ sum-x panel 0, sum-y panel 0, cx, cy,
         pt-dist (sp 3.0 4.0) (sp 0.0 0.0),
         max-radius panel (sp 0.0 0.0) 0,
         max-radius panel (sp 0.0 0.0) 0 * 3.2 ]

  halo-want : List Real
  halo-want = [16.0, 16.0, 4.0, 4.0, 5.0, 10.0, 32.0]

 Sixteen points on the circle, which is `glow-sides` and not a written-down 16.

  circle-count-got : List Integer
  circle-count-got = [list-length (glow-circle 0.0 0.0 10.0 0), glow-sides, tire-sides]

  circle-count-want : List Integer
  circle-count-want = [16, 16, 16]

Section: Behind The Eye

 Neither gradient shape is clipped; both are dropped whole the moment any corner
 reaches the near plane. The plane is at 0.4 and the test is `<=`, so a vertex
 sitting exactly on it counts as behind.

  behind-got : List Boolean
  behind-got =
    [ any-behind [v3 10.0, v3 20.0, v3 30.0] 0,
      any-behind [v3 10.0, v3 0.2, v3 30.0] 0,
      any-behind [v3 10.0, v3 0.4, v3 30.0] 0,
      any-behind [v3 0.41] 0,
      any-behind [] 0 ]

  behind-want : List Boolean
  behind-want = [False, True, True, False, False]

 The midpoint of two body points, which is where a lamp sits on a panel edge.

  mid-got : List Real
  mid-got =
    let m = mid-vec (Vec3 { right = 1.0, forward = 2.0, height = 3.0 }) (Vec3 { right = 5.0, forward = 8.0, height = 11.0 })
    in [m.right, m.forward, m.height]

  mid-want : List Real
  mid-want = [3.0, 5.0, 7.0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "td-box   " box-got box-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "td-bottom" bottom-got bottom-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "td-axles " axles-got axles-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "td-naxle " axle-count-got axle-count-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "td-face  " face-got face-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "td-carry " face-carry-got face-carry-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "td-tied  " tied-got tied-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "td-slack " slack-got slack-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "td-small " small-got small-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "td-halo  " halo-got halo-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "td-circle" circle-count-got circle-count-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "td-behind" behind-got behind-want)
  end
